A||||22||fieroluke||Phantom wipe||||5||
Z||000000||fieroluke||06-21-2001||03:27 AM||||Hi everybody,<P>my 85GT suffers from the "phantom wipe", i.e. is one single wipe if I turn on the ignition. It doesn't do it again until you let it sitting for 30 minutes or more. It's really annoying. Other than that the wipers are fully functional.<P>What I did so far:<P>Swapped module in wiper motor with 3 known good modules from other Fieros: no change.<P>Swapped column switch with one from the Fiero Store (used, from an 87 Fiero, didn't want to spend $90 for a new one): no change.<P>Swapped column switch with one from an 84 (without the "ratchet" stops for delay): problem gone.<P>So, logic would dictate that the switch is bad, right? Well, both (ratcheting) switches work on another Fiero, and I measured them electrically, and they're fine. I really like the ratcheting delay positions, and I want to keep them, so swapping in the 84 switch is out of the question. But I don't want to order yet another expensive switch and find that this is not the problem.<P>The problems seems to be common not only with the Fiero (the wiper system is the same for a lot of GM cars of the period), but I have not found an explanation for the phenomenon yet.<P>Can anyone offer some insight?<P>Best regards,<P><BR>-Oliver<P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||06-21-2001||03:35 AM||||I wish I could help, but I'm pretty sure my phantom wiper problem is in my stalk (only because it's happened a few times when I use the cruise control).  Best of luck to you.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000002||fieroluke||06-21-2001||04:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>I wish I could help, but I'm pretty sure my phantom wiper problem is in my stalk (only because it's happened a few times when I use the cruise control).  Best of luck to you.<P>MM</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, if it happens when the steering column shakes or if you touch the stalk, it's in the switch.<P>Thanks for the comments!<P>-Oliver||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cozmo||06-21-2001||06:51 AM||||When I pressure washed the front compartment of my GT it started doing that too... I <BR>let it bake in the sun for a while and it went away.  I know that doesn't explain your results using different stalks, but it may give you an idea or two...<BR>Maybe an exorcisim???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ???<BR><B>C02M0</B>||||reg||3
Z||000004||Denny||06-21-2001||07:36 AM||||I have two 86's, an SE and a GT. Both have the delay wiper option. The GT behaves itself, the SE decides randomly (is it programmed like that?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) to "phantom" wipe. I have yet to find the cause.<P>If you figure it out, Oliver, I'd really like to know.<P>May the Force be with you!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Denny (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieroluke||06-21-2001||08:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cozmo:<BR>Maybe an exorcisim???   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ???<BR><B>C02M0</B>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, I think holding a lighter to a fender might cure the problem (and all others); I heard Fieros are also a pretty sight when burning with their typical, bright orange flames. But I'll try the exorcism firstm ;-)<P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||fieroluke||06-21-2001||08:53 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Denny:<BR>[B]I have two 86's, an SE and a GT. Both have the delay wiper option. The GT behaves itself, the SE decides randomly (is it programmed like that?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) to "phantom" wipe. I have yet to find the cause.<P>If you figure it out, Oliver, I'd really like to know.<P>If it's really random, it's probably the switch (something loose in there, and makes contact every now and then). Mine only does it exactly once when power is first applied to the wiper motor/control board.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Denny||06-21-2001||09:40 AM||||It can go for months with no phantom wipe, then it does it exactly one wipe. I think you may have a point about something being loose, since it has happened when just driving down the road.<P>Good luck with yours!  Let us know if you find it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mr.Frosty||06-21-2001||10:18 AM||||I've also had that problem and haven't figured it out.Since I don't drive mine in bad weather I just disconnected the module.I think it happened when I washed the front compartment and I got some of the switches wet.Eventually I'm going to have to figure it out,if I ever want to use my windshield washers.Mine does it constantly every couple of minutes and doesn't stop.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000010||btoth||06-21-2001||10:30 AM||||My wipers turn on when I use the turn-signal (but only sometimes) and sometimes when I turn the car on or hit a bump.  These are all completely random though.  I had a new control arm installed and that fixed nothing.<P>It does seem to be a common GM problem, at least in the 80's cars that I've been in.<P>I installed a push-button toggle switch to where the windsheild wiper fuse is so I can turn them off easily on nice sunny days and turn them on briefly when I need them.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fieroluke||06-21-2001||12:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>My wipers turn on when I use the turn-signal (but only sometimes) and sometimes when I turn the car on or hit a bump.  These are all completely random though.  I had a new control arm installed and that fixed nothing.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's not the arm, it's the switch inside the column that's bad! Installing a new control arm is wasted money! I bet if you install a new switch in the column, you can discard that extra switch you added...<P>Best regards,<P>-Oliver<P><P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000012||V8Dreamer||06-21-2001||04:30 PM||||Rockcrawl had that same problem with the 87 GT auto he just sold, you may want to ask him if he knows what it is......although now that I think of it, if he knew he probably would've fixed so, but it's worth a try I guess.<P>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000013||pherder||06-21-2001||05:39 PM||||Here's my take on this.<P>I have had <B>MANY</B> Fieros that had the phantom wipe.  In <B>EVERY</B> case, replacing the switch in the column solve it.<P>I am talking about 6~8 Fieros that had the problem.  I even had several Fieros that didn't have the problem!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I just got thru changing the switch out in my 86SE.  The switch that came with the car had an interesting problem.  When I started the engine, the wipers would come on and continue to wipe <B>UNTIL</B> I turned <B>ON</B> the wipers and then turned them off.<P>I replaced the switch with one out of a column I had laying around and got rid of the problem of the wipers coming on until I turned them off and got the phantom wipe instead!!  One wipe after starting the engine and only when the car was "cold" ... i.e. sitting for "a while".<P>I ordered a wiper switch from Flow Chevrolet (gmpartsdirect.com) for $73, got about a week after I ordered it and installed it yesterday.<P>No phantom wipe.  <P>The only interesting part is the delay wipe is a <B>LOT</B> longer and not as easily adjusted as the switch in my other Fieros.<P>I can live with it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000014||Jay||06-21-2001||07:06 PM||||Phantom wipe?  I thought this was going to be a thread on a new way to wipe your a$$, you know the old ways...the fold or crumple, one wipe or two, finger or whole hand. ROTFLOL. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||PBJ||06-21-2001||08:45 PM||||likely cause is due to bad or poor ground a G201.  This ground is shared by the washer pump and the turn signal and brake lights.  What is happens is this poor ground causes a back feed from lights through washer pump and wiper pulse board think for a moment you requested washer operation and sweeps the wipers.<P>And yes the switch to could cause phantom sweep but first clean up G201 or unplug washer pump and drive for a few days to see if phantom wipe is gone.  <P>G201 is behind center of I/P||||reg||1
Z||000016||ka4nkf||06-22-2001||12:17 AM||||I have this problem also for a long time. The GM manual says there is a Delay Module under the dash on the right hand side of the sterring collum by the collum bracket. I have looked and I cannot find this module. It is supposed to be a silver box with fins on it. The book says unplug the two cables going to it and plug the two cables together and you will regular wipers without delay. I have looked and cannot find this module, and I believe this could be the problem, Because it acts a delay problem. If anyone finds this module please post here on the forum where it is located.||||reg||1
Z||000017||fieroluke||06-22-2001||04:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ka4nkf:<BR><B>I have this problem also for a long time. The GM manual says there is a Delay Module under the dash on the right hand side of the sterring collum by the collum bracket. I have looked and I cannot find this module. It is supposed to be a silver box with fins on it. The book says unplug the two cables going to it and plug the two cables together and you will regular wipers without delay. I have looked and cannot find this module, and I believe this could be the problem, Because it acts a delay problem. If anyone finds this module please post here on the forum where it is located.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>At least I can help here: This module is only present on the 1984 Fiero, from 1985 on they eliminated the separate module, and instead moved a bare circuit board right into the wiper motor. They didn't update all of the Helms manuals though, some some places still contain the old information.<P>Best regards,<P>-Oliver<BR><P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||fieroluke||06-22-2001||04:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pherder:<BR><B>Here's my take on this.<P>I have had [b]MANY</B> Fieros that had the phantom wipe.  In <B>EVERY</B> case, replacing the switch in the column solve it.<P>Paul[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hi Paul,<P>interesting, that's what I thought too. Especially since going back to that '84 switch made the problem go away. But why does the other switch I have show the same problem, but not on a different car? I think several factors have to coincide before the phantom shows its ugly head. Maybe a bad ground *and* a bad switch? Curing either of the causes would then eliminate the problem.<P>If that is so, a lot of people can save $80 each for a new switch, if a simple solution is available.<P>I'll keep experimenting...<P>Thanks,<P>-Oliver<P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||LZeitgeist||06-22-2001||10:34 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>One wipe after starting the engine and only when the car was "cold" ... i.e. sitting for "a while".<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>YES!!!!! My Formula has done this for as long as I have had it. <P>So are you guys talking about the switch that you actually rotate on the end of the stalk, or a different switch in the bowels of the steering column?||||reg||1
Z||000020||pherder||06-22-2001||11:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B>So are you guys talking about the switch that you actually rotate on the end of the stalk, or a different switch in the bowels of the steering column?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>switch in the bowels of the steering column<P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000021||ka4nkf||06-22-2001||01:49 PM||||One more question! has anyone had this problem with wipers without delay?||||reg||1
Z||000022||pherder||06-22-2001||04:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ka4nkf:<BR><B>One more question! has anyone had this problem with wipers without delay?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR><B>GOOD</B> question!!  I have never owned a Fiero <B>without</B> the delay!<P>Anyone?<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000023||fieroluke||06-25-2001||03:30 AM||||Hi guys,<P>okay, I did diagnostic #1 that was proposed here, i.e. disconnect the washer pump (easy enough). No change. Symptom's still there.<P>If the pump motor's resistance has anything to do with it though, maybe the resistance is too high or something? I do have a replacement (GM) washer pump on there, with the new style connector. <P>It looks like there is a capacitor on the board that discharges with the module powered off, and when you turn on the ignition, it charges again, and things are back to normal. That's the only explanation I have as to why it only happens when the car has been sitting for a while.<P>Anyway, an interesting observation: If I turn<BR>on the ignition for just a split second (before the wipers have a chance to react and start to move), and then turn on the ignition normally a second later, the phantom often doesn't take place! (the on-off-on timing is a bit tricky as it seems)<BR>Looks like turning on the ignition for a short moment does charge that capacitor, but because I turn it off immediately, the wipers don't start moving. Hmm.<BR>Of course I usually forget to do that, so I get the phantom wipe most mornings anyway. Can anybody verify if this only works with *my* phantom wipe?<P>What I'd really like would be a simple fix, like adding a resistor or a diode or something somewhere to prevent having to buy another ignition switch (if that really is the core problem). I'm about to design a new wiper board, with a uP, that way I can have the wipers delay longer when the car is below a certain speed... We'll see...<P>I really wonder why there is virtually nothing to be found (at least no solution or explanation) on the entire internet on this very common problem with so many GM cars...<P>Best regards,<P>-Oliver<BR><P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg"> ||||reg||13
